<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Liao dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Khitan Liao, Liao</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Liao dynasty was a state founded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khitans">Khitans</a> that ruled northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> from the tenth to the early twelfth century. It was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty</a>'s principal northern rival, and the Treaty of Chanyuan in 1005 obliged the Song to make annual payments to the Liao. The Liao was overthrown by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a> Jin in the 1120s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty.jpg" alt="Liao dynasty" width="320" height="225" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SY · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Liao_circuits.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Khitan Liao</b> state absorbed most of its territory and employed former Balhae officials, refugees strengthened the new Korean kingdom of Goryeo, and the Jurchens, who later founded the Jin dynasty, descended from Mohe peoples of the region.</blockquote><a href="/empire/balhae">Balhae · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Its greatest early test came from the Khitan <b>Liao dynasty</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/goryeo">Goryeo · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Jin dynasty was founded by the Jurchen, a Tungusic people of the forests and river valleys of Manchuria who lived by farming, hunting, fishing and stock-raising and paid tribute to the Khitan <b>Liao dynasty</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/jin-dynasty-1115-1234">Jin dynasty (1115-1234) · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Qara Khitai was the western remnant of the <b>Liao dynasty</b> of the Khitans, a Sinicized Para-Mongolic people of eastern Mongolia and north-east China, whose empire the Jurchen-led Jin dynasty conquered between 1114 and 1125.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qara-khitai">Qara Khitai · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…heartland after the fragmentation of the Five Dynasties and Ten Kingdoms period, though it never recovered the Sixteen Prefectures around modern Beijing held by the Khitan <b>Liao dynasty</b>, and under the Chanyuan Treaty of 1005 it agreed to costly annual payments of silk and silver to the Liao.</blockquote><a href="/empire/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>His kinsman Li Jiqian refused to submit: he took Yinzhou in 985, paid tribute to the <b>Liao</b> from 986, married into Khitan nobility in 989 and in 990 accepted from the Liao emperor the title King of the Xia State, defying and placating the Song by turns until…</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-xia">Western Xia · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
